practice.solution:
export type Article = { readonly id: string; title: string; body?: string; tags?: string[] };

export type Draft<T> = { -readonly [K in keyof T]?: T[K] };

function show(article: Article | null): string {
  if (article === null) return "not ready";
  return `${article.id}: ${article.title} [${(article.tags ?? []).join(", ")}]`;
}

export function finalize(d: Draft<Article>): Article | null {
  if (d.id === undefined || d.title === undefined) return null;
  return { ...d, id: d.id, title: d.title };
}

const draft: Draft<Article> = {};
draft.title = "Mapped types";
console.log(show(finalize(draft)));

draft.id = "a1";
draft.tags = ["typescript", "types"];
console.log(show(finalize(draft)));

steps.body:
`as` after the key gives it a new name: `[K in keyof T as NewName]`. This `as` only renames keys. It has nothing to do with type assertions. Two kinds of `NewName` cover most code you'll read. The first is a template literal type, written in backticks like a template string, which builds the new name from the old one:

```typescript types
type Account = { owner: string; balanceCents: number };

type Getters<T> = { [K in keyof T as `get${Capitalize<string & K>}`]: () => T[K] };
type AccountGetters = Getters<Account>;
//   ^? type AccountGetters = { getBalanceCents: () => number; getOwner: () => string; }
```

`Capitalize` uppercases the first letter, so `owner` becomes `getOwner`. `string & K` keeps only the string keys, since `keyof` can also give number and symbol keys. The Template Literal Types lesson covers these types fully. The bubble sorts the new keys by name, and the order of keys in a type never matters.

The second is `never`: a key renamed to `never` is dropped. `T[K] extends string ? K : never` keeps a key only when its value is a string. That's a conditional type, an if for types, which the Conditional Types lesson covers.

examples.code:
type Account = { owner: string; balanceCents: number };

type Getters<T> = { [K in keyof T as `get${Capitalize<string & K>}`]: () => T[K] };

function accountGetters(account: Account): Getters<Account> {
  return {
    getOwner: () => account.owner,
    getBalanceCents: () => account.balanceCents,
  };
}

const getters = accountGetters({ owner: "Ada", balanceCents: 12050 });
const balance = getters.getBalanceCents();
//    ^? const balance: number

console.log(getters.getOwner());
console.log(`$${(balance / 100).toFixed(2)}`);

type Product = { sku: string; name: string; priceCents: number; inStock: boolean };

type StringFields<T> = { [K in keyof T as T[K] extends string ? K : never]: T[K] };

type ProductText = StringFields<Product>;
//   ^? type ProductText = { sku: string; name: string; }

function search(products: Product[], field: keyof ProductText, text: string): string[] {
  return products
    .filter((p) => p[field].toLowerCase().includes(text.toLowerCase()))
    .map((p) => p.name);
}

const products: Product[] = [
  { sku: "MUG-01", name: "Blue mug", priceCents: 1299, inStock: true },
  { sku: "TEA-02", name: "Green tea", priceCents: 650, inStock: false },
  { sku: "MUG-03", name: "Tall mug", priceCents: 1499, inStock: true },
];

console.log(search(products, "name", "mug").join(", "));
console.log(search(products, "sku", "tea").join(", "));

examples.explanation:
For each key, the condition asks whether its value is a string. `sku` and `name` keep their names, while `priceCents` and `inStock` are renamed to `never`, which drops them. So `keyof ProductText` is `"name" | "sku"`, and `p[field]` is always a `string`, which is why `toLowerCase()` compiles. A field that isn't text is a type error:

```typescript error
type Product = { sku: string; name: string; priceCents: number; inStock: boolean };
type StringFields<T> = { [K in keyof T as T[K] extends string ? K : never]: T[K] };

function search(products: Product[], field: keyof StringFields<Product>, text: string): string[] {
  return products.filter((p) => p[field].includes(text)).map((p) => p.name);
}

search([], "priceCents", "12");
```

`tsc` reports TS2345: `"priceCents"` isn't one of `"name" | "sku"`.

